From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Toon Verwaest <verwaest@chromium.org>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 19:12:37 +0200
Subject: [parser] Report kStrictEvalArguments in destructuring sub-patterns

In strict mode, `IsValidReferenceExpression(result)` returns `false` for
`eval` and `arguments` because `IsAssignableIdentifier(result)` is
`false`.

Previously, `ParsePossibleDestructuringSubPattern` gated its identifier
branch on `IsValidReferenceExpression(result)` and fell through to
recording `kInvalidDestructuringTarget` when `result` was `eval` or
`arguments` in strict mode. As a result, `"use strict"; [eval] = []`,
`"use strict"; let [eval] = []`, and `"use strict"; ({a: eval} = {})`
reported `Invalid destructuring assignment target` instead of
`Unexpected eval or arguments in strict mode` (unlike shorthand
`"use strict"; ({eval} = {})`).

Similarly, `ParseProperty` (`case Token::kEllipsis`) checked
`!IsValidReferenceExpression(expression)`, causing
`"use strict"; ({...eval}) => {}` and
`"use strict"; function f({...eval}) {}` to report
`` `...` must be followed by an identifier in declaration contexts ``
even though `eval` is an identifier.

Check `impl()->IsIdentifier` directly in both places and record
`kStrictEvalArguments` as a pattern error when `!IsAssignableIdentifier`
in `ParsePossibleDestructuringSubPattern`.

